--TEST--
Core\Math's `int`-parameter members are asked about both ends of `int` and refuse only where the *answer* has no room, while its `float`-parameter members cannot be asked about those ends at all — `rule:types/conversion` and `rule:types/arithmetic` and docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The depth claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*, and the bound is not
// one: `Core\Math` splits into members declared over `int` and members
// declared over `float`, and the two families stop in different places. An
// `int` member stops where its own *answer* leaves `int` — one value for
// `abs`, one pair for `intDiv`, one pairing for `gcd` — while a `float` member
// stops where its *argument* does, at `rule:types/conversion`'s 2^53, long before either
// end of `int` is in sight. A member picked from the wrong family therefore
// looks total on every row anyone would write and refuses the value the caller
// actually had.
//
// What each member answers on a chosen row is read off a line in
// math-magnitude-sign-and-the-two-extremes.nvst, and the intDiv/mod refusals
// are in math-int-div-and-mod-at-the-edges-of-their-arms.nvst; neither asks
// which family a member is in.

int $max = Core\Math::INT_MAX;
int $min = Core\Math::INT_MIN;

// 1. `sign` is the one integer member with no refusal in the whole range: it
//    reads a bit rather than computing a magnitude, so both ends and both of
//    their neighbours answer. This is the baseline the rest part from.
echo "sign: ", Core\Math::sign($min), Core\Math::sign($min + 1), "|",
     Core\Math::sign($max - 1), Core\Math::sign($max), "\n";

// 2. `abs` refuses at exactly one value in the type, and the last accepted one
//    is its neighbour — named together, since a member that stopped one entry
//    early prints plausibly against either half alone. The top end is total:
//    the asymmetry is `int`'s, not the member's.
echo "abs: ", Core\Math::abs($min + 1) as int, "|", Core\Math::abs($max) as int, "\n";
int $absRefused = 0;
try {
    echo Core\Math::abs($min) as int;
} catch (ArithmeticError $noMagnitude) {
    $absRefused = 1;
}
echo "abs at int min refused ", $absRefused, "\n";

// 3. `gcd` and `lcm` take the whole range on both sides and walk it over
//    `i128`, so what refuses is the conversion of the *answer* back: a bound
//    in neither argument. `$min`'s magnitude is one past `int`'s largest, so
//    every pairing whose answer *is* that magnitude refuses, and for `gcd`
//    there are exactly two — `($min, 0)`, because every integer divides zero,
//    and `($min, $min)`. Each is named beside a neighbouring pairing that
//    answers, including the one a single step away.
echo "gcd: ", Core\Math::gcd($min, 2), "|", Core\Math::gcd($min, $max), "|",
     Core\Math::gcd($min, $min + 1), "
";
int $gcdRefusals = 0;
try {
    echo Core\Math::gcd($min, 0);
} catch (ArithmeticError $noRoomZero) {
    $gcdRefusals = $gcdRefusals + 1;
    echo "gcd at (int min, 0): ", $noRoomZero->message, "
";
}
try {
    echo Core\Math::gcd($min, $min);
} catch (ArithmeticError $noRoomSelf) {
    $gcdRefusals = $gcdRefusals + 1;
}
// `lcm` is the mirror image at zero, and inverted everywhere else: it answers
// before any multiplication happens, so the pairing that defeats `gcd` is the
// one `lcm` never computes — while `($min, 1)`, which `gcd` answers with `1`,
// is a product `lcm` has no room for.
echo "lcm: ", Core\Math::lcm($min, 0), ", gcd refusals ", $gcdRefusals, "
";
int $lcmRefused = 0;
try {
    echo Core\Math::lcm($min, 1);
} catch (ArithmeticError $noProduct) {
    $lcmRefused = 1;
}
echo "lcm at (int min, 1) refused ", $lcmRefused, "
";

// 4. The other family. Every member below is declared `float`, so an `int`
//    argument reaches it only through `rule:types/conversion`'s one implicit widening,
//    which is exact or throws — and 2^53 is where it stops, which is 1024
//    times short of `int`'s own ends. The sweep is counted over five members
//    rather than read off one, so a member that grew a conversion of its own
//    fails here while still looking right on its own line.
int $edge = 9007199254740992;
int $over = 9007199254740993;
array<int> $magnitudes = [$edge, 0 - $edge, $over, 0 - $over, $max, $min];
foreach ($magnitudes as int $mag) {
    int $accepted = 0;
    try { Core\Math::floor($mag); $accepted = $accepted + 1; } catch (Throwable $noFloor) { $accepted = $accepted; }
    try { Core\Math::ceil($mag); $accepted = $accepted + 1; } catch (Throwable $noCeil) { $accepted = $accepted; }
    try { Core\Math::truncate($mag); $accepted = $accepted + 1; } catch (Throwable $noTrunc) { $accepted = $accepted; }
    try { Core\Math::mod($mag, 2.0); $accepted = $accepted + 1; } catch (Throwable $noMod) { $accepted = $accepted; }
    try { Core\Math::hypot($mag, 0.0); $accepted = $accepted + 1; } catch (Throwable $noHypot) { $accepted = $accepted; }
    echo "float family accepted ", $accepted, "/5\n";
}

// 5. The refusal is about the argument and says so, which is what tells it
//    apart from the `int` family's three above: those name a member and its
//    answer, this one names a conversion the member never saw. The class is
//    the same — `rule:types/arithmetic`'s `ArithmeticError`, which the *same* rule raises
//    at a `callable`'s parameter, pinned in
//    arr-a-callback-float-parameter-widens-an-int-and-stops-at-2-53.nvst — so
//    the message is all that tells the two refusals apart.
try {
    echo Core\Math::floor($over);
} catch (ArithmeticError $tooWide) {
    echo "widening: ", $tooWide->message, "\n";
}

// 6. The last accepted magnitude arrives *whole* rather than rounded to
//    something nearby, on both signs — an acceptance that silently lost the
//    low bit would still count as an acceptance above.
echo "2^53 whole: ", Core\Math::mod($edge, 4503599627370496.0), "|",
     Core\Math::mod(0 - $edge, 4503599627370496.0), "|",
     Core\Math::floor($edge), "\n";
--EXPECT--
sign: -1-1|11
abs: 9223372036854775807|9223372036854775807
abs at int min refused 1
gcd: 2|1|1
gcd at (int min, 0): Core\Math::gcd's answer does not fit an `int`
lcm: 0, gcd refusals 2
lcm at (int min, 1) refused 1
float family accepted 5/5
float family accepted 5/5
float family accepted 0/5
float family accepted 0/5
float family accepted 0/5
float family accepted 0/5
widening: cannot convert `int` 9007199254740993 to `float`
2^53 whole: 0|-0|9.007199254741E+15
